<code>assert index >= 0 && index <= \old(this.size()) => (obj == null ? this.get(index) == null : this.get(index).equals(obj));</code>
--------------------
<code>assert index >= 0 && index <= \old(this.size()) => (obj == null) => (this.get(index) == null) || this.get(index).equals(obj);</code>

However, the above statement seems to have a logical error in its structure. The correct interpretation should be that if the object is not null, the element at the specified index after insertion should equal the object. Here's the corrected version:

<code>assert index >= 0 && index <= \old(this.size()) => (obj == null || this.get(index).equals(obj));</code>

This ensures that if the index is within the valid range and the object is not null, the element at that index after insertion should equal the object. If the object is null, no specific assertion is made about the element at that index, as the comment does not specify behavior in that case.
